<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>Satellite data for Earth observation has become essential for
  monitoring natural and human processes. The increasing volume of
  publicly accessible remote sensing data brings many opportunities, but
  also operational challenges, that continue to limit its use across a
  broad range of users. Processing chains promote the use of this data
  by automating the tedious work of repetitively processing large
  datasets. They are important for fully leveraging the repetitive
  nature of the imagery by creating time series (TS) generated from past
  satellite missions, and enabling near-real-time automated updates as
  new images are acquired.
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>
  allows for heavy processing of Sentinel-2 (S2) satellite images by
  offering functions dedicated to the management of large databases. Its
  operation is quite straightforward, with object-oriented programming,
  for easy manipulation of data and functions. It was developed for the
  needs of operational scientific research programs aimed at producing
  short-term environmental indicators for early warning systems, which
  required large-scale, high-resolution spatial environmental data over
  long periods and at high temporal frequencies. Its free and
  open-source nature enhances its accessibility, and broadens its
  potential range of use.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>The high availability of large open access satellite image datasets
  currently makes it possible to consider operational analyses covering
  vast areas. TS of environmental indices from Earth observation (EO)
  data are essential for monitoring and predicting land surface
  dynamics. Assessing the changes of individual pixel values ​​over time
  through various spectral indices is a common approach in remote
  sensing research
  (<xref alt="Hislop et al., 2018" rid="ref-HislopU003A2018" ref-type="bibr">Hislop
  et al., 2018</xref>). These TS capture seasonal environmental
  fluctuations and local dynamics that may be linked to human practices
  such as deforestation
  (<xref alt="Gao et al., 2020" rid="ref-GaoU003A2020" ref-type="bibr">Gao
  et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Laag &amp; Pilario, 2026" rid="ref-LaagU003A2026" ref-type="bibr">Laag
  &amp; Pilario, 2026</xref>), agriculture
  (<xref alt="Bégué et al., 2018" rid="ref-BegueU003A2018" ref-type="bibr">Bégué
  et al., 2018</xref>), water quality
  (<xref alt="Pahlevan et al., 2022" rid="ref-PahlevanU003A2022" ref-type="bibr">Pahlevan
  et al., 2022</xref>), fire monitoring
  (<xref alt="Huang et al., 2016" rid="ref-HuangU003A2016" ref-type="bibr">Huang
  et al., 2016</xref>;
  <xref alt="Verhegghen et al., 2016" rid="ref-VerhegghenU003A2016" ref-type="bibr">Verhegghen
  et al., 2016</xref>), but also natural trends or exceptional weather
  events such as floods or cyclones, which are heavily influenced by
  climate change
  (<xref alt="Sanyal &amp; Lu, 2004" rid="ref-SanyalU003A2004" ref-type="bibr">Sanyal
  &amp; Lu, 2004</xref>). Since 2015, the exploitation of TS of EO data,
  using the free S2 optical satellite constellation of the European
  Space Agency’s (ESA) Copernicus program, has marked an important step,
  bringing a significant gain compared to previous satellites in terms
  of spatial and temporal resolutions (up to 10 meters and 5 days
  respectively). These characteristics have already proven their worth
  in a wide range of fields, such as the mapping of land-cover
  (<xref alt="Phiri et al., 2020" rid="ref-PhiriU003A2020" ref-type="bibr">Phiri
  et al., 2020</xref>), the impact of natural disasters
  (<xref alt="Mouquet et al., 2020" rid="ref-MouquetU003A2020" ref-type="bibr">Mouquet
  et al., 2020</xref>) or the mapping of habitats
  (<xref alt="Poursanidis et al., 2019" rid="ref-PoursanidisU003A2019" ref-type="bibr">Poursanidis
  et al., 2019</xref>). Users of S2 images benefit from pre-processed
  data, orthorectified and atmospherically corrected, directly usable
  for their applications. They also have free access to powerful ESA
  tools designed for advanced analysis, such as
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://step.esa.int/main/download/snap-download/">SNAP</ext-link>
  and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://step.esa.int/main/snap-supported-plugins/sen2cor/">Sen2Cor</ext-link>.
  Combined with the ease of access to S2 data through a
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/data-collections/copernicus-sentinel-missions/sentinel-2">web
  portal</ext-link> and the
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://dataspace.copernicus.eu/">Copernicus
  Data Space Ecosystem (CDSE)</ext-link> API, this has opened up very
  interesting prospects for scientific research and the development of
  operational products. Such a context has encouraged the development of
  tools to process complete TS of images, particularly for the serial
  production of environmental indices. This article introduces
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>,
  a processing chain designed to download, process, and manage large
  volumes of S2 data covering broad temporal and spatial extents.
  Sen2Chain was designed to generate and manage large amounts of EO
  data, by automating and parallelizing the processing of S2 images, and
  producing consistent TS of radiometric indices.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>Sen2Chain is developed in Python and runs similarly to existing
  dedicated S2 image processing applications, such as
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/ranghetti/sen2r">sen2r</ext-link>
  (<xref alt="Ranghetti et al., 2020" rid="ref-RanghettiU003A2020" ref-type="bibr">Ranghetti
  et al., 2020</xref>) whose development based on the R language has
  stopped since 2023, complementing it with more specific features. For
  instance, it integrates an advanced image download tool, supports easy
  management of an image and derivative database, allows automatic
  extraction of statistics in regions of interest (ROI), automates the
  processing of vast geographical areas and time periods.</p>
  <p>The rapid increase in the number of online geospatial data catalogs
  and cloud-based solutions has led to the emergence of new standards
  with specifications aimed at standardizing the way geospatial assets
  are exposed online and queried. The
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://stacspec.org/en">STAC
  standard</ext-link> provides a standardized framework for cataloging,
  searching, and describing geospatial datasets, while
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/opendatacube/odc-stac">ODC-STAC</ext-link>
  enables these datasets to be loaded into Python as data structures
  compatible with the major scientific computing libraries. This
  approach offers considerable flexibility for developing custom
  processing workflows and exploiting multi-sensor datasets. Tools that
  leverage these standards include
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/ESDS-Leipzig/cubo">Cubo</ext-link>
  and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/e-sensing/sits">SITS</ext-link>
  software programs which enable the construction of homogeneous
  spatiotemporal data cubes from multiple data sources, including STAC
  catalogs and Google Earth Engine, thereby facilitating their use
  within scientific computing environments such as Python
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://docs.xarray.dev/en/stable/"><monospace>xarray</monospace></ext-link>,
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.dask.org/"><monospace>Dask</monospace></ext-link>,
  and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://numpy.org/"><monospace>NumPy</monospace></ext-link>).
  This approach is particularly well suited to applications in data
  science, remote sensing, and machine learning. Also, while these tools
  are primarily designed for cloud access, loading, and data
  organization, they do not natively provide a dedicated preprocessing
  workflow for Sentinel-2 imagery or automated generation of derived
  indicators. Sen2Chain was developed as a processing pipeline
  specifically dedicated to Sentinel-2 time series. Beyond image
  download, it automates the main preprocessing steps required to
  produce analysis-ready data, including cloud mask handling, spectral
  index computation, and the generation of homogeneous time series. This
  level of automation addresses the needs of users working with large
  volumes of Sentinel-2 imagery who require ready-to-use products for
  analysis without having to develop or integrate multiple independent
  processing tools.</p>
  <p>The tool also stands out from Google Earth Engine (GEE), one online
  processing platform that also allows processing of large volumes of
  data from EO satellites. GEE offers many advantages and is
  increasingly used worldwide for scientific research
  (<xref alt="Velastegui-Montoya et al., 2023" rid="ref-Velastegui-MontoyaU003A2023" ref-type="bibr">Velastegui-Montoya
  et al., 2023</xref>), especially by users with limited resources
  (<xref alt="Johary et al., 2023" rid="ref-JoharyU003A2023" ref-type="bibr">Johary
  et al., 2023</xref>;
  <xref alt="Vijayakumar et al., 2024" rid="ref-VijayakumarU003A2024" ref-type="bibr">Vijayakumar
  et al., 2024</xref>). But it is not without constraints either and
  includes limitations such as limited free storage space, restrictions
  on freely available processing tools, limitations in terms of data
  sharing with other users and rules of use, intellectual property
  rights and confidentiality governed by a private company
  (<xref alt="Amani et al., 2020" rid="ref-AmaniU003A2020" ref-type="bibr">Amani
  et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Padarian et al., 2015" rid="ref-PadarianU003A2015" ref-type="bibr">Padarian
  et al., 2015</xref>).</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>Sen2Chain was designed with simplicity and minimalism in mind,
  ensuring ease of use for a wide audience, including those with limited
  resources and non-experts in programming or advanced cloud processing.
  It is aimed at users who, in their various applications, typically
  work with remote sensing images and time-series data that can be
  easily visualized and analyzed using standard consumer software such
  as Excel or QGIS, or subjected to more advanced analysis using R or
  Python. The design thinking process focused on producing simple,
  lightweight data stored directly in a system directory structure,
  thereby ensuring high accessibility from the local hard drive while
  facilitating easy management, updating, reuse, and dissemination.</p>
  <p><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>
  is entirely developed in Python, one of the most widely used open
  source languages ​​for scientific and geospatial applications, to
  efficiently perform various functions
  (<xref alt="Canty, 2025" rid="ref-CantyU003A2025" ref-type="bibr">Canty,
  2025</xref>;
  <xref alt="Kaya et al., 2019" rid="ref-KayaU003A2019" ref-type="bibr">Kaya
  et al., 2019</xref>). Packages and tools specific to the exploitation
  of S2 imagery were used, such as
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/CS-SI/eodag">EODAG</ext-link>
  for data download,
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://step.esa.int/main/snap-supported-plugins/sen2cor/">Sen2Cor</ext-link>
  for atmospheric corrections, and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/DPIRD-DMA/OmniCloudMask">OmniCloudMask</ext-link>
  (OCM) for cloud detection.
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>
  was designed to handle the large volumes of data involved in
  processing S2 images with high spatial resolution, high temporal
  frequency, and global coverage. The minimum requirements to run the
  tool are a personal computer, workstation or laptop, server or virtual
  machine running under Linux to launch the various commands and modules
  in Python. Some functions are very demanding in terms of computing
  resources, such as Sen2Cor and OCM which rely on external software and
  libraries to perform specific processing. Performance will
  consequently improve with greater RAM and CPU (multi-core) resources,
  and OCM will also benefit from GPU acceleration for cloud detection
  using
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/nvidia/cuda-python">CUDA</ext-link>.
  Regarding storage capacity, a complete Sentinel-2 tile, currently
  comprising over 750 dates across the entire temporal range, occupies
  between 500 GB and 1 TB of disk space. The database, stored locally on
  the user’s workstation or on a computing server, can easily contain
  tens of thousands of images representing tens of terabytes of data.
  Consequently, features have been developed within Sen2Chain to
  rigorously manage the local database and prevent exceeding the
  machine’s storage capacity.</p>
  <sec id="key-features">
    <title>Key features</title>
    <p>After opening an account and obtaining right credentials from one
    of the various data providers, which are mostly free but may be
    subject to quotas, Sen2Chain allows the retrieval of S2 images from
    online catalogs
    (<xref alt="[fig:figure_1]" rid="figU003Afigure_1">[fig:figure_1]</xref>).
    Users can choose either to download the raw L1C images to produce
    their own L2A images, or to download L2A images directly from the
    catalogues, thereby reducing resources and processing time. After
    querying the online catalogs, downloads are performed by geographic
    zones, using the identifiers of the tiles of interest, and
    user-defined maximum cloud cover and temporal extent. The L2A images
    are then used to generate cloud masks and spectral indices (e.g.,
    brightness, vegetation, and humidity indices), whose values ​​can be
    extracted from areas of interest for the needs of different
    applications. Sen2Chain can produce different versions of cloud
    masks, either directly using cloud-related classes derived from the
    L2A Scene Classification product, or using the open-source OCM
    library, which employs sensor-independent deep learning models and
    offers excellent cloud detection performance for Sentinel-2 data
    (<xref alt="Wright et al., 2025" rid="ref-WrightU003A2025" ref-type="bibr">Wright
    et al., 2025</xref>).</p>
    <fig>
      <caption><p>Key features of the Sen2Chain tool, including
      downloading, storing and processing Sentinel-2 images and their
      products<styled-content id="figU003Afigure_1"></styled-content></p></caption>
      <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="jpeg" xlink:href="./scheme_sen2chain_joss.jpg" />
    </fig>
    <p><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>
    uses object-oriented programming to facilitate and automate tasks.
    The <monospace>Tile</monospace> class handles image operations at
    the tile level, while the <monospace>Job</monospace> class has been
    implemented to facilitate the management of the image database and
    the execution of all the different processing tasks. Jobs enable a
    set of tiles to be processed as a complete workflow (from
    downloading to index production) in a recurring and automated
    manner. The <monospace>TimeSeries</monospace> class allows users to
    extract radiometric index data over specific sites, from point or
    polygon vector files, and timeframes, with customizable cloud masks.
    This class operates similarly to the QGIS
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://plugins.qgis.org/plugins/temporalprofiletool">Temporal/Spectral
    Profile Tool</ext-link> plugin, while also allowing the process to
    be automated across a large number of polygons over a user-defined
    time period, and without requiring the creation of data cubes or
    virtual rasters and enabling the rapid export of results as CSV
    files for external use. All processing steps of the chain have been
    designed and optimized to handle large volumes of data, in
    particular through parallelization using the Python modules
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/threading.html"><monospace>threading</monospace></ext-link>
    (downloading) and
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://docs.python.org/fr/3/library/multiprocessing.html"><monospace>multiprocessing</monospace></ext-link>
    (computing cloudmasks, indices, and TS extraction) when possible to
    fully leverage multiple processors of the computing machine,
    distributing the load and reducing the computation time.</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="tutorials-and-documentation">
    <title>Tutorials and Documentation</title>
    <p>Sen2Chain provides comprehensive
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://sen2chain.readthedocs.io/en/latest/">documentation</ext-link>
    including installation instructions, cookbook, and extensive details
    of the different classes and methods hosted on the opensource
    platform
    <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://about.readthedocs.com/">ReadTheDocs</ext-link>.</p>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>The development of
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>
  was initiated in 2017 for research projects S2-Malaria (funded by CNES
  - TOSCA program, 2016-2018) and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.spaceclimateobservatory.org/climhealth">ClimHealth</ext-link>
  (accredited by the Space Climate Observatory and funded by CNES,
  2020-2021), using environmental data in epidemiological surveillance
  systems, and
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.ird.fr/projet-renovrisk-impact-evaluation-des-dommages-des-risques-cycloniques-dans-le-bassin-de-locean">ReNovRisk-Impact</ext-link>
  (funded by FEDER INTERREG V Ocean Indien 2014-2020), mapping the
  impacts of cyclones
  (<xref alt="Mouquet et al., 2020" rid="ref-MouquetU003A2020" ref-type="bibr">Mouquet
  et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Tulet et al., 2021" rid="ref-TuletU003A2021" ref-type="bibr">Tulet
  et al., 2021</xref>). This tool has been under continuous development
  since its creation, with new improvements and features progressively
  added to meet research needs. It has been used in several research
  projects within different research units
  (<xref alt="Blanchard et al., 2024" rid="ref-BlanchardU003A2024" ref-type="bibr">Blanchard
  et al., 2024</xref>;
  <xref alt="Evans et al., 2025" rid="ref-EvansU003A2025" ref-type="bibr">Evans
  et al., 2025</xref>;
  <xref alt="Mouquet, 2026" rid="ref-MouquetU003A2026" ref-type="bibr">Mouquet,
  2026</xref>), and currently several operational online tools using
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://framagit.org/espace-dev/sen2chain">Sen2Chain</ext-link>
  make it possible to track the evolution of health risks in real time
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://leptoyangon.geohealthresearch.org/">ClimHealth
  - Leptospirosis Yangon</ext-link>), to extract TS data on areas of
  interest
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://web.seas-oi.org/sen2extract/">Sen2Extract</ext-link>)
  or to monitor the evolution of mangroves
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://mangmap.org/en/">Mangmap</ext-link>).</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>No generative AI tools were used in the development of this
  software, the writing of this manuscript, or the preparation of
  supporting materials.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements">
  <title>Acknowledgements</title>
  <p>Thanks to UMR Espace-Dev colleagues - Jean-François Faure, Laurent
  Demagistri, Christophe Charon, Thibault Catry - and UMR Mivegec
  colleagues - Andres Garchitorena, Mauricianot Heriniaina, Michelles
  Evans, Felana Ihantamalala for their help testing, deploying, and
  promoting the tool.</p>
</sec>
</body>
